# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2 have "vicious → burden" and "honest → virtue_others" and "virtue_others → ¬servants_thine"; boethius-1.logic.r2.3 has "vicious → burden" and "burden → ¬servants_happy" and "honest → virtue_others" and "virtue_others → ¬servants_thine"; they differ when only "a long train of servants makes thee happy", "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are honest", "the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and exceeding dangerous to their master" and "what the servants have of worth is other men's virtue" are true, which the first allows
boethius-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "a long train of servants makes thee happy", "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are honest", "the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and exceeding dangerous to their master" and "what the servants have of worth is other men's virtue" are true and "the servants are really thine, to be counted in the sum of thy possessions" is false. Is S2, "Why, if they behave viciously, they are a ruinous burden to thy house, and exceeding dangerous to their own master; while if they are honest, how canst thou count other men's virtue in the sum of thy possessions?", true then?
boethius-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r2 has nothing from it; boethius-1.logic.r2.2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.3 have "beautiful → ¬beauty_thine"; they differ when only "these things are beautiful in their own nature" and "their beauty is anything to thee" are true, which the first allows
boethius-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2.2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "these things are beautiful in their own nature" and "their beauty is anything to thee" are true. Is S5, "While if they are beautiful in their own nature, what is that to thee?", true then?
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-1.logic.r2 has "vicious ∨ honest" and "burden → ¬servants_thine" and "all_thine → servants_thine" and "pleasing_without ∧ ¬precious_from_counted → ¬beauty_thine"; boethius-1.logic.r2.2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.3 have "vicious ∨ honest" and "all_thine → servants_thine"; they differ when only "the servants behave viciously", "the servants are honest", "the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and exceeding dangerous to their master" and "the servants are really thine, to be counted in the sum of thy possessions" are true, which the second allows
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: boethius-1.logic.r2 takes for granted "if the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and exceeding dangerous to their master, then it is not so that the servants are really thine, to be counted in the sum of thy possessions"; boethius-1.logic.r2.2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.3 do not
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the servants are a ruinous burden to thy house and exceeding dangerous to their master, then it is not so that the servants are really thine, to be counted in the sum of thy possessions"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U2: boethius-1.logic.r2 takes for granted "if (they would have been not less pleasing in themselves though never included among thy possessions and it is not so that they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches), then it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee"; boethius-1.logic.r2.2 and boethius-1.logic.r2.3 do not
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps boethius-1.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (they would have been not less pleasing in themselves though never included among thy possessions and it is not so that they derive their preciousness from being counted in thy riches), then it is not so that their beauty is anything to thee"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
boethius-1.logic.r2: argue/verdicts: valid as boethius-1.logic.r2 reads it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict
